[ChapterName "Vienna Gambit — 3...d5 Main Line"]
[ChapterIndex "1"]

1. e4 {White controls the central d5 square and opens pathways for the queen and light-squared bishop.} 1... e5 2. Nc3 {The Vienna Game. White develops the queen knight, securing e4 and preparing the sharp f2-f4 strike without blocking the f-pawn.} 2... Nf6 3. f4! {The Vienna Gambit! A forceful central challenge undermining Black's e5 anchor.} 3... d5! {The most principled grandmaster defense, counter-striking in the center.} 4. fxe5 {White liquidates central tension and drives the f6 knight into an advanced post.} 4... Nxe4 5. Qf3! {The aggressive Steinitz-Paulsen continuation! White pins the e4 knight, targets f7, and prepares rapid queenside castling.} (5. Nf3 Bc5 6. d4 Bb4 7. Bd2 Nxd2 8. Qxd2 O-O 9. a3 Be7 10. Bd3 {The classical alternative with solid central development.}) 5... f5 {The main defense, anchoring the e4 knight with the f-pawn.} (5... Nxc3 6. bxc3 Be7 7. d4 O-O 8. Bd3 c5 9. Ne2 Nc6 10. O-O {White holds a commanding central pawn structure and kingside attack.}) (5... Nc6 6. Bb5 Nxc3 7. dxc3 Qh4+ 8. g3 Qe4+ 9. Qxe4 dxe4 10. Bf4 {White has the superior endgame with active bishop development.}) 6. d3 Nxc3 7. bxc3 d4 8. Qg3! {Activating the queen on the kingside, eyeing g7 and unpinning the light-squared bishop.} 8... dxc3 9. Be2 Nc6 10. Nf3 Be6 11. O-O Qd7 12. Be3 {White completes development with complete central command, safe king safety, and lethal kingside attacking potential.} *

